    cooksNotes: "A citrus-garlic marinade stands in for a beef carne asada marinade on chicken — same char and acidity, no red meat. Steps are resequenced so the peppers and onion cook and rest first, the chicken sears last off a pat-dried surface (blotting the marinade off first lets it actually sear instead of steam), and the vegetables return to the pan only to reheat during the chicken's rest — the original order cooked the vegetables after the chicken rested, so the chicken was going cold on the cutting board by the time everything was plated. Tortillas now get an explicit warming step (dry skillet or open flame, 20-30 seconds per side), matching this file's dn2-chicken-tinga and dn2-shrimp-fajitas, instead of the old passive 'warm corn tortillas' adjective with no actual warming action. Half the lime is now reserved for a finishing squeeze rather than all of it going into the marinade, since marinade acid mostly cooks off during searing — the same convention used on dn2-chicken-tinga and dn2-chicken-fajita-bowl. 'Charred' is now scoped to the grill option specifically; a home skillet sauté in 5-6 minutes realistically gets tender-crisp with good color, not true char, unless the pan is very hot and the vegetables are left mostly undisturbed. The olive oil is split — 1 teaspoon into the marinade, the rest reserved for the pan — since the original recipe put all of it in the marinade and left nothing to actually sauté or sear with. Salt is added for the first time in this recipe (1/4 teaspoon kosher salt in the marinade, plus a pinch on the peppers and onion) — previously this card carried zero salt anywhere, the review board's most emphatic finding. This is a home-composed 10-ingredient dish with no single product label, so all five nutrition figures are marked estimated (not verified) and are resummed component-by-component from USDA raw values, following this file's established convention (see dn2-chicken-fajita-bowl, dn2-turkey-taco-skillet, dn2-shrimp-fajitas): the original 569 kcal / 54 g protein / 5.0 g sat fat figures appear to have applied cooked/roasted chicken-breast density to the 160 g raw weight, the same methodology error already fixed on this file's other raw-chicken cards — unqualified 'Chicken breast' plus a gram quantity means raw weight here, and 160 g raw breast (~120 kcal, ~22.5 g protein per 100 g) lands well below the old figures. Cotija is the largest single sodium and saturated-fat contributor in the dish (~150 mg sodium, ~1 g sat fat for 1 tbsp, a branded-label-style estimate, since cotija sodium varies meaningfully by brand); olive oil and raw chicken breast each contribute comparatively little saturated fat on their own. Corrected sodium lands around 284 mg once that new added salt and cotija's real sodium are both counted — still comfortably inside low-sodium territory (this file's tagged low-sodium dinners mostly run 240-290 mg), so the tag holds.",
